An introduction to UCC-UnrealCreative™
Chapter 1-Base rules
This language consists of a few key parts, starting with variables, a variable must be defined at the top of the script or before use, a variable is defined like this "[variable name]  [variable type],  [variable name] [variable type]."a variable can have 4 states on the base language, 1 Float, a float is a number that has the ability to switch between integer based numbers like 1 and 2, and it also has the ability to go into decimals, like 1.1, 1.2 ect, this number can also be rounded like an integer. 2 Integer, an integer is a number with no decimals, so it can only do 1, 2 ect. This number can be rounded but to a certain point, since there are no decimals you can use "Round [Variable] [Number to round to]" to round it to the nearest number, it will round to the closest number. 3 Bool, a bool is basically binary but with a wrapper, bool provides true or false values, it has main uses for bool oriented functions. 4 String, string is text, if you want to display text you can convert it to string, you can use "to string [variable]" to make any value string, you can display string in a bunch of ways, from prints to logs. Next up is functions, a function can be called upon using "run([function name])", a function looks like this "def [function name]([code here])", a function will run whatever is in the function. when defining a function, it does not run, but when defining a function you do have to put it near your variables. Next is memory, you can ask the system for ram, you gain that ram but you have to manually get rid of that ram, same goes for gpu and CPU usage, unless in your settings you specifically turn off manual usage. once you do, you can still ask for more resources like normal, but your resources that you asked for doesn't behave like system controlled resources, system controlled resources ask when needed and gets rid of it when not needed. Next and last thing of this chapter, syntax. Syntax is the most important thing in a language, all lines have a terminator, a terminator tells the language that you are done with that line, the terminator is ".",  and you can take breaks in code by typing ",", you only take breaks when declaring variables but it can have outside usage. 
Chapter 2-File Functions and libraries.
This language includes things like file I/o and libraries, file I/o means it has the ability to create, delete and change files, when you install a library, you need somewhere to store it so you don't have to reinstall it every time you want to use it, so when you install a library, the language knows that you wanted the library in your system and under the compiler, so it write a file, it is only seen to the language, when it installs a library it installs it under"[compiler name]/libs/[lib]" and it accesses that lib when you ask for it, by going down that directory.
a lib is a library of code, if it can be made in ucc? it can be a lib! someday we want to make it so our source language (C) can be made into libs. libs are sourced from github, hosting platforms and more, and a lib is conprised of a few key parts, definitions and names and how they work, a lib would look like this "Echo. def Echo(INPUT("Echo(Variable)") Print(Echo).", and downloading a lib would look something like this "InstallLib(source("HTTPS://example.com/lib"))" and it gets routed to your ib dir for use, it is recommended that you install a lib before using at all. 
Chapter 3-Math
lets start by defining how math works, in this language mat goes from left to right, so 1+2==variable1, there are a few math symbols, we have +, -, *, /, %, and for comparison functions, =, <, >, <= and => which output boolean values, you cant print out the output of a number by doing this "1+2=Print(=)" but this only works if the math function is directly outside of the math and build specifically like that, otherwise it will fail since "=" isnt a variable. the math works on int and float. 
Chapter 4-Loops and Special cases
lets dive right into loops. a loop loops lines of code or functions until it is done for however long you want, a loop would look like this "Loop=>5([blocks of code]).". a special case are cases where you go mroe in depth on a function, so instead of using boolean math, you would do something like "if(age<18 else(if(age=18 else(print"you are a minor).).).)." but while it is possible to use it like this, it is not recommended. 
Chapter 5-How LBL works and how to change it
LBL stands for line by line, it is how all languages read code but for very rare use cases you can change it to read it par by par, a par is a line of code a long of code going until its period, lbl is slower but more thorough, while pbp is faster but not as good, it is recommended that you let you code take its time. PBP and be activated through the settings.
